Contact Dermatitis (bullae)
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Erythematous plaques corresponding to exposure, Intense pruritus/burning, Geometric or linear configurations

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-013
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, blistering skin eruption with intense pruritus severely disrupting sleep and daily functioning.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute, rapidly evolving, intensely pruritic blistering rash developing over the forearms and hands, leading to significant distress, sleep deprivation, and fear of a severe systemic allergic reaction. Urgent assessment was required to differentiate contact dermatitis from a severe systemic blistering disorder (e.g., bullous pemphigoid, Stevens-Johnson Syndrome) and to provide immediate potent topical or systemic anti-inflammatory relief before the next available routine appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My arms are covered in giant blisters that itch so badly I feel like tearing my skin off. It's spreading everywhere."
History of Present Illness: The patient developed an intensely pruritic and burning skin eruption 24 hours ago, approximately 12 hours after clearing dense brush and weeds in their garden. The rash began as well-demarcated erythematous plaques but has rapidly progressed to significant vesicle and bullae formation. The lesions are localized exclusively to the bilateral dorsal forearms and hands. The patient notes intense oozing and crusting following accidental vesicle rupture. The extreme pruritus has completely prevented sleep. There is a complete lack of systemic symptoms such as fever or airway compromise.
Functional Status: Independent, but manual dexterity is limited by blistering and swelling on the hands.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute distress due to pruritus and sleep deprivation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Highly uncomfortable, constantly scratching at the forearms, visibly fatigued.
Physical Exam: The bilateral dorsal forearms and hands exhibit striking, intensely erythematous plaques. There are multiple linear and geometric configurations of the rash, tracing the exact path where plant matter brushed against the skin. Superimposed on the erythema are numerous tense vesicles and larger bullae, some of which have ruptured, leaving weeping, crusted erosions. The rash precisely corresponds to the site of suspected allergen exposure. No mucosal involvement. No generalized urticaria.
Environmental Observations: Gardening gloves and tools visible near the back door.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No new oral medications or suspected drug eruptions.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Acute Allergic Contact Dermatitis (Rhus dermatitis / Plant-induced) - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Herpes Zoster (Shingles) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The bilateral, symmetrical nature of the rash, the linear geometric pattern of exposure, and the predominance of intense pruritus over severe neuropathic pain.

Bullous Impetigo - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The clear history of plant exposure, the intense pruritus (impetigo is typically painless), and the linear, non-clustered arrangement of the bullae.

Autoimmune Bullous Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The hyper-acute onset, direct correlation with an environmental exposure, and lack of generalized or mucosal involvement.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, severe, blistering cutaneous eruption directly following a known environmental exposure, exhibiting the classic linear geometry and extreme pruritus of allergic contact dermatitis. Triage correctly identified the rapid blistering and extreme distress as requiring immediate evaluation to rule out a severe systemic or infectious blistering disorder. The clinical presentation is localized but highly symptomatic. Urgent after-hours intervention to prescribe a short course of systemic corticosteroids and potent topical therapy was necessary to rapidly arrest the severe inflammatory cascade, break the itch-scratch cycle, and prevent extensive secondary bacterial infection from the excoriated bullae, avoiding a likely Emergency Department presentation for intractable itch and pain overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Prednisolone 25mg daily for 5 days due to the severity and blistering nature of the reaction. Prescribed topical Betamethasone dipropionate 0.05% ointment for localized application. Advised cool compresses and oral antihistamines (Loratadine 10mg) for symptomatic itch relief.
Positive Behavioral Change: By applying cool, damp washcloths to the blisters instead of scratching, you will physically numb the nerve endings and instantly reduce the itching without breaking the skin. By taking the oral steroid exactly as prescribed each morning, you will rapidly shut off the severe allergic reaction from the inside, stopping new blisters from forming and allowing you to sleep through the night.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 5 days to assess the resolution of the rash and the weaning of the short-course steroids.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If you develop swelling around your face or mouth, difficulty breathing, or if the clear fluid in the blisters turns into thick, yellow, foul-smelling pus with a fever, present to the Emergency Department immediately.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Blistering reactions can occasionally worsen over the first 48 hours before the medication takes full effect.
Clinical Course: The intense itching should begin to subside within 24 hours of starting the oral steroids. The blisters will slowly dry up and crust over the next week.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if you develop any signs of a widespread severe allergic reaction, such as facial swelling or difficulty breathing.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your immune system severely overreacted to the plant oil touching your skin. The oral steroids will calm down this aggressive immune response, while the cream treats the local inflammation.
Actionable Steps: Do not pop the blisters; the skin roof protects the raw area underneath from infection. Wash all gardening clothes and tools in hot soapy water to remove residual plant oils.

